<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Bourbon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bourbon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Bourbon was a European royal dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and, from the early eighteenth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Spanish Bourbons introduced administrative and fiscal reforms in the empire. Their weakness during the Napoleonic invasion of Spain helped trigger the independence movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="House of Bourbon" width="286" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Royal_Coat_of_Arms_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Crown’s last war ended with the <b>Bourbon</b> victory at the Battle of Almansa in 1707 and the fall of Barcelona in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the childless Charles II died in 1700, the War of the Spanish Succession brought the <b>Bourbon</b> Philip V to the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Bourbon</b> dukes the minister Guillaume du Tillot promoted agriculture, manufactures and learning, founding an academy of fine arts and the Palatine Library, and from 1768 Giambattista Bodoni ran the ducal printing house, whose typefaces became famous across Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip V’s accession ended Habsburg rule in Spain and provoked the War of the Spanish Succession (1701–1714), after which the <b>Bourbons</b> kept Spain and the Americas while the Netherlands and the Italian possessions passed mostly to Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1705–1706 much of Aragon, with Catalonia and Valencia, declared for the Habsburg claimant Archduke Charles against the <b>Bourbon</b> Philip V. After the Bourbon victory at the Battle of Almansa on 25 April 1707, Philip V abolished the fueros of Aragon and Valencia by right of conquest in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>That May Giuseppe Garibaldi’s Expedition of the Thousand landed in Sicily and overthrew the <b>Bourbons</b> in the south, and Sardinian troops marched through the Papal Marche and Umbria to meet him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
